# Upstream source pin for spiderfoot.  Shell-sourceable: debian/rules does
# ". debian/upstream.pin" and uses the variables directly.  Keep it to plain
# VAR=value assignments with no expansion, no quoting tricks and no commands.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS, WHY A COMMIT SHA AND NEVER A TAG, WHY A SHA256 AS
# WELL, AND THE FULL ADJUDICATION PROCEDURE FOR A SHA256 MISMATCH:
#
#   see packages/metagoofil/debian/upstream.pin.
#
# That file is the canonical statement of the scheme for every "tier C" tool
# in this repo and it is not duplicated here.  It is not optional reading: it
# is what tells you how to distinguish a benign GitHub re-compression from a
# tampered download BEFORE you re-pin anything, and "just make the build
# green" is the exact action an attacker is relying on.  Substitute this
# package's repository (smicallef/spiderfoot) and the variables below wherever
# that procedure names metagoofil's.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
# READ THIS: THE TAG AND THE PINNED COMMIT ARE NOT THE SAME THING
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The packaging request for this tool named "tag v4.0, commit
# 0f815a203afebf05c98b605dba5cf0475a0ee5fd".  Those two do not agree, and the
# discrepancy was resolved in favour of the SHA.  Resolved 2026-09-17 with
# `git ls-remote --tags https://github.com/smicallef/spiderfoot`:
#
#   refs/tags/v4.0  -> b9c345de5b085debc7444fc10e0e26e7745df5f2
#                      (lightweight tag; 2022-04-07, "Update README.md to
#                      reflect 4.0, bump to 4.0 in the various places.")
#
#   pinned below    -> 0f815a203afebf05c98b605dba5cf0475a0ee5fd
#                      (2023-11-05, "sfwebui: Update jQuery 3.6.0 to 3.7.1
#                      (#1822)"), a later commit on master.
#
# Both objects exist in the repository; neither is a typo.  The SHA was
# preferred for three reasons, in order of weight:
#
#   1. The dependency analysis that authorised this package's relaxed pins was
#      performed against the SHA's requirements.txt, not the tag's.  The two
#      differ, and only the SHA's file matches the analysis:
#
#        pin                tag v4.0          0f815a2 (pinned)
#        lxml               >=4.6.3,<5        >=4.9.2,<5
#        PyPDF2             >=1.26.0,<2       >=1.28.6,<2
#        publicsuffixlist   >=0.7.9,<0.8      >=0.9.3,<0.10
#        pyyaml             >=5.4.1,<6        >=6.0.0,<7
#        dnspython          >=2.1.0,<3        >=2.3.0,<3
#
#      Packaging the tag would mean shipping a tree whose dependency wall was
#      never examined.
#
#   2. A commit SHA is immutable and a tag is not; the instruction to pin this
#      package was itself "pin the SHA, never the tag".
#
#   3. The pinned commit is strictly newer and carries security-relevant
#      fixes the tag does not, notably the bundled jQuery 3.6.0 -> 3.7.1 bump
#      in the web UI that is the pinned commit's own subject.
#
# The shipped VERSION file at the pinned commit reads "SpiderFoot 4.0.0" and
# spiderfoot/__version__.py agrees, so the Debian version 4.0 is accurate for
# this tree.  Upstream cut no release after v4.0; the pinned commit is
# post-4.0 master.  If that matters to a future maintainer, the honest
# description is "4.0 plus 19 months of master", and a version such as
# 4.0+git20231105 would be the conventional way to say so -- it was not used
# here only because the package was specified as version 4.0.
#
# If you bump this pin, re-read upstream's requirements.txt at the new commit
# and regenerate ../requirements.txt, then update the table in
# debian/README.Debian.  Upstream's pins drift and the relaxation decisions
# recorded there are only valid against the pins they were made from.

UPSTREAM_URL=https://github.com/smicallef/spiderfoot/archive/0f815a203afebf05c98b605dba5cf0475a0ee5fd.tar.gz
UPSTREAM_SHA256=d5eaa95d612795e7c1da309324e98c9e1ac1c67afc5597c38f211f76bbac4ba0
UPSTREAM_COMMIT=0f815a203afebf05c98b605dba5cf0475a0ee5fd
# NOTE: UPSTREAM_REF is NOT the v4.0 tag -- see the section above. It is a
# bare token with no spaces, parentheses or quotes on purpose: debian/rules
# sources this file with /bin/sh, so anything fancier is a syntax error that
# fails the build after the whole virtualenv has already been installed.
UPSTREAM_REF=post-v4.0-master
UPSTREAM_FETCHED=2026-09-17
